// Test: bugfixes/sem004_iterate_requires_a_list
// Grammar: iterate_statement
// Semantic: SEM004 — `iterate x in value` walks a list (or a range); any
//   other statically typed value is rejected with a hint.
// Fixed in: compiler 0.33.174
// Expected output:
// Expected error:
//   error[SEM004]: Cannot iterate over a value of type string
//   sem004_iterate_requires_a_list.cln:22:
//   help: `iterate` walks the elements of a list or a range
//
// Regression: iterating a non-list gave the loop variable a type variable
// that was never resolved, and the loop read garbage.
//
// Fixed: this pin iterated an `any` (from json.decode). type-system.md §9
// says an `any` is not type-checked, and a JSON array is a `list<any>`
// (stdlib-reference.md §8), so iterating an `any` is accepted and walks the
// array (bugfixes/json_array_any_as_list.cln). A string is still rejected.

start:
	string data = "12"
	iterate x in data
		printl(x)
